/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "76681192-0bc8-5fd9-98fc-5fd254bc93fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124956 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x, y, z) = xy + yz + xz$ and $r(t) = ti + e^{t}j + ek$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f10e177e-2926-5cde-8e9a-4b0a99b2ff4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + 3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t) = ⟨t^{2}, t + 1,-t⟩$ at the point $t = 1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125249 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x, y) = \\ln xy$ and $r(t) = \\cos ti + t^{2}j$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1061 12678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f62c8fc-daae-562a-a01e-851a375ec7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1061 12681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea045ecb-90e1-5dc8-804d-711207e5f86c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1061 12682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef0f7fd7-782e-55cb-8008-f28d770450ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1061 12684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cde1eff3-797d-5f65-a385-6296a6f32db3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1061 30935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87b55278-4bb6-5686-b4ef-178d5036b236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1061 30936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4850b0cc-54ab-5a26-9440-b0d1cf757680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1061 30937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd19c878-fabf-5b99-9507-9a32fb862324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1064 "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1065 2652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06350cc4-9c76-53db-a464-43e93f2fcc2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1067 "The Derivative as a Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}t)(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1065 4967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "673ff670-99f1-5416-aa83-5c4c8bf0e4f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1065 4968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "15062cce-eede-545a-ae5d-e02432a0ba33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1067 "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule" #edb/re