f 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284584 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\,j+t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78b6efe3-1fb5-5655-97e2-03def53db36e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284584 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284585 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284586 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284587 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321194 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05130439-f8ad-50ab-8